WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.980
Quinn just moved its largest model yet from preview to general availability, giving Builders a 2.4 trillion parameter MoE with native image and video input at published per token pricing. This is Models &amp; Agents, Episode 130. Alibaba's Quinn team released Quinn 3.8 max, a 2.4 trillion parameter mixture of experts model that accepts text, image, and video across a 1 million token context.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:45.320
DeepSeek launched an ultra low-cost model that research firms say undercuts every well-known competitor on inference price. OpenAI claims its next model, referred to as Astra, produced breakthroughs on 10 previously unsolved math problems.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:01:02.740
Cogent AI released VR1, a purpose-built cyber reasoning model that ships with its own enterprise intrusion benchmark. The Quinn release continues the rapid cadence of large open-weight releases that closed the gap with proprietary systems we tracked yesterday.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:23.760
Alibaba's Quinn team moved Quinn 3.8 max from preview to general availability today. They published per token pricing while open weights are scheduled for next week. The 2.4 trillion parameter MoE accepts text, image, and video input with a 1 million token context window.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:45.980
No benchmark table was released with the announcement, so direct comparisons to other frontier models remain limited for now. Builders working on multimodal agents or long-context retrieval can now test the model through the existing Quinn API without waiting for weights. Watch for the weight drop next week and any early independent e-vals that surface.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:02:00.980
DeepSeq released a new model positioned as the cheapest option among well-known models to run, according to research firm analysis. The announcement follows the pattern of DeepSeq repeatedly targeting inference cost as the primary differentiator.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:16.780
No parameter count, context length, or benchmark numbers were disclosed in the initial coverage. Builders focused on high volume, low margin applications should test the new endpoint this week to measure real cost against current providers.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:28.440
OpenAI stated that its upcoming model, referred to internally as Astra, produced solutions to 10 mathematical problems that had seen no progress for at least a decade.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:40.940
The claim centers on long horizon reasoning, rather than standard benchmark saturation. No details on model size, training approach, or whether the solutions were independently verified have been released.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:49.200
Teams working on formal mathematics or theorem-proving tooling should monitor the next public release for concrete evaluation data.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:59.980
Cogent AI released VR1, a model post-trained specifically for cybersecurity rather than acquiring capability as a side effect of general coding.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:11.040
The release includes intrusion bench, which scores agents on completed enterprise intrusions, and the Cogent AI harness, a governed runtime for security agents.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:17.920
The approach targets explicit attack path composition and verification instead of broad tool use.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:25.840
Security teams, evaluating autonomous red team tooling, now have a dedicated benchmark and runtime to test against.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:34.200
Simon Willison asked for the ability to trigger custom MCP tool calls directly through chat GPT voice on iPhone.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:46.180
The request highlights the gap between current voice interfaces and the growing ecosystem of personal MCP servers. No implementation details or timelines were provided.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:54.100
Developers building personal automation stacks should watch for any official MCP voice integration in the coming weeks.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:02.680
A recent analysis examines the remaining barriers to fully autonomous agents focusing on reliability and oversight requirements.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:09.260
It notes that current systems still need human escalation paths for complex or high stakes decisions.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:14.980
Teams deploying agents in production should continue prioritizing control plane designs.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:23.880
Another piece argues that production grade agent deployments require an explicit control plane for monitoring governance and rollback.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.980
It positions this layer as a prerequisite rather than an optional add on.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:36.320
Operators planning multi agent systems should treat control plane design as a first class architectural decision.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:48.420
Andre Carpathi shared a browser playable version of the Pelican on bicycle test originally posted by Simon Willison along with a teaser for a GTA style Hobbiton scene.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:57.540
The demo lets users fork and run the test locally. The post also references an earlier six month LLM retrospective.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.720
Builders interested in visual consistency testing now have an immediately runnable example to extend.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:16.700
GSK entered a research collaboration with relation therapeutics worth up to 110 million dollars to generate large scale data sets of human cell responses to genetic changes and drug interventions.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:24.500
The data will train AI models for drug discovery. The agreement expands existing work between the two organizations.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:30.960
Teams working on biological foundation models should track the resulting data sets when they become available.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:39.240
Simon Willison noted that the newly available Quinn 3.8 max appears distinct from the earlier preview version.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:45.000
The clarification helps developers distinguish between the two checkpoints when running comparisons.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:52.080
Anyone benchmarking Quinn models this week should confirm they are using the GA release rather than the preview.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.840
Okay, let's pop the hood on boundary expanded dynamic early exit.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:03.600
Everyone talks about early exiting in reasoning models as if it is simply a matter of checking for I'm done tokens.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:14.400
In practice, the technique requires inspecting a wider set of natural boundaries in the generated trace and learning which layers carry the most predictive signal at each boundary.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:26.420
The core idea starts with sentence and paragraph breaks plus explicit self-doubt phrases then adds repeated answer completion probes to label whether the prefix is already sufficient.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:36.140
Because different layers encode different kinds of uncertainty the system learns a small subset of informative probe layers rather than reading every hidden state.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:43.500
This adds a modest calibration step at training time but avoids the latency of full layer inspection at inference.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:53.720
The quality gain holds on math and coding benchmarks while cutting generated tokens by roughly 15 to 25% on the tested Quinn 3 reasoning models.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.300
The savings shrink once the model is already highly optimized.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:08.620
When your workload is dominated by long, self-verifying traces and you can afford a one-time calibration pass, boundary expanded early exit is worth testing.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:16.020
If your traces are short or the model family changes frequently, the engineering overhead outweighs the benefit.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:26.460
Test Quinn 3.8 max through the current API for any multimodal retrieval or long context agent task you have been running on smaller models.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:36.500
Run the browser fork of Carpathi's Pelican on bicycle demo to experiment with visual consistency checks before building your own evaluation harness.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:44.240
Benchmark deep seeks new low-cost endpoint against your current provider on a representative high volume workload.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:52.120
Explore the intrusion bench tasks released with Cogen VR1 if you are evaluating autonomous security tooling.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:56.900
Open weights for Quinn 3.8 max are expected next week.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:04.140
Further details on open AI's astramodel and its math problem results are anticipated in coming announcements.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:11.040
Additional low-cost model releases from Chinese labs are likely to continue the price compression trend.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:17.540
More agent control plane tooling and governance runtimes are expected as production deployment scale.

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:27.580
Before we go, the next wave of low-cost releases should clarify how far Chinese labs can push inference economics without new architectural disclosures.

